From: minimal (W9, TSan gate)
Subject: [PATCH] mcp_message.h: request::generate_id() is process-global, make it atomic

minimal-mcp patch: generate_id()'s `next_id` is a function-local static, but
that means it is shared by EVERY mcp::stdio_client (and mcp::request) in the
process, not just one connection's -- request ids across unrelated
McpConnections all draw from the same counter. Two connections building
requests concurrently (a restarted server's old, still-live connection and
its replacement, both real during a restart) raced on the plain `int`
post-increment (TSan-reported: mcp_message.h:126, mcp::request::generate_id).
Uniqueness across connections was never guaranteed or required, only a
monotonically-assigned id within one call's request -- an atomic increment
keeps that property and closes the race.
--- a/include/mcp_message.h
+++ b/include/mcp_message.h
@@ -12,6 +12,7 @@
 #include <string>
 #include <vector>
 #include <map>
+#include <atomic>
 #include <functional>
 #include <memory>
 #include <stdexcept>
@@ -122,7 +123,10 @@ struct request {
 private:
     // Generate a unique ID
     static json generate_id() {
-        static int next_id = 1;
-        return json(next_id++);  // Explicitly create json to ensure type consistency
+        // minimal-mcp patch: atomic, not a plain int -- this counter is
+        // shared process-wide across every mcp::stdio_client instance, and
+        // two connections building requests concurrently raced on it.
+        static std::atomic<int> next_id{1};
+        return json(next_id.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed));
     }
 };
